abin on river left. Easy to walk on river right.\n\nChannel-wide strainer (pine tree, partially submerged) in first gorge drop (after stone bridge). Definitely scout.","user":{"name":"Jake Weller"}},{"id":"101247386","title":null,"post_date":"2010-10-16 04:00:00+00","detail":"10-16-10 There is a strainer in Gilsum Gorge at the first drop after the bridge. It did not look passable to me. I didn't see a left line around that huge rock and the log go's across the entire right line. Make sure you scout this.","user":{"name":"Jon P Hansen"}},{"id":"100973630","title":null,"post_date":"2009-07-27 04:00:00+00","detail":"We ran the Upper Ash at 4.7 on 7/26/09.\nThere's a big pine tree almost all the way across the river right above the last bridge before the take-out. It had not been there in Aril 2009.\n It can be easily seen from the road & the river. Scouting, it looked easily passable on river left. We made it through, but it was a lot tighter than it had seemed. At higher levels the water would've been pushy enough to make it very dicey. The right bank is high at that spot so it may not widen the channel.","user":null}],"photos":[{"id":"8751","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3585","description":"","caption":"Surprise Rapid","poi_name":"Surprise","subject":"Mike Brindamour","author":"Mark Lacroix","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8751","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8751"}},{"id":"10464","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8383","description":"","caption":"Upper Ashuelot","poi_name":"unknown","subject":"","author":"Bob Dunn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10464","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10464"}},{"id":"10465","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8383","description":"","caption":"Gilsum Gorge","poi_name":"Gilsum Gorge","subject":"Punkin Head","author":"Bob Dunn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10465","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10465"}},{"id":"10466","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8383","description":"","caption":"Gilsum Gorge","poi_name":"Gilsum Gorge","subject":"unknown paddler","author":"Bob Dunn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10466","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10466"}},{"id":"10467","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8383","description":"","caption":"Gilsum Gorge","poi_name":"Gilsum Gorge","subject":"unknown paddler","author":"Bob Dunn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10467","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10467"}},{"id":"10463","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8383","description":"The length of the rapid is condensed in this photo by a telephoto lens.","caption":"Surprise Rapid","poi_name":"Surprise","subject":"","author":"Bob Dunn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10463","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10463"}},{"id":"10468","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"8383","description":"A large boulder blocks the river's path in Gilsum Gorge.","caption":"Gilsum Gorge","poi_name":"Gilsum Gorge","subject":"unknown paddlers","author":"Bob Dunn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/10468","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/10468"}},{"id":"RWhICEeVuHDOOcZgStdva","ext":"jpg","post_id":"cYujkYhnWOQiOSoBnuH84","description":"","caption":"Gilsum Gorge Entrance","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Bill Durr","poi_id":"bRbfI9y7rFBgIP5saepR0","photo_date":"2023-12-20T20:28:28.000Z","updated_at":"2023-12-21T20:28:28.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/RWhICEeVuHDOOcZgStdva","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/RWhICEeVuHDOOcZgStdva"}},{"id":"Ro1AfowUisFBc6yIlcFk7","ext":"jpg","post_id":"cYujkYhnWOQiOSoBnuH84","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Bill Durr","poi_id":"nw8Q8Cq6aDwtYtJeOfuag","photo_date":"2023-12-20T20:28:28.000Z","updated_at":"2023-12-21T20:28:28.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/Ro1AfowUisFBc6yIlcFk7","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/Ro1AfowUisFBc6yIlcFk7"}},{"id":"IwvyMwYVbxsI1AGKlA8Nf","ext":"jpg","post_id":"cYujkYhnWOQiOSoBnuH84","description":"","caption":"Gilsum Gorge Crux Move","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Bill Durr","poi_id":"bRbfI9y7rFBgIP5saepR0","photo_date":"2023-12-20T20:28:28.000Z","updated_at":"2023-12-21T20:28:28.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/IwvyMwYVbxsI1AGKlA8Nf","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/IwvyMwYVbxsI1AGKlA8Nf"}},{"id":"QnB9Hhqgi8M3dDCyFD69p","ext":"jpg","post_id":"cYujkYhnWOQiOSoBnuH84","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Bill Durr","poi_id":"nw8Q8Cq6aDwtYtJeOfuag","photo_date":"2023-12-20T20:28:28.000Z","updated_at":"2023-12-21T20:28:28.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/QnB9Hhqgi8M3dDCyFD69p","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/QnB9Hhqgi8M3dDCyFD69p"}}],"detail":{"id":"1159","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"### Thanks to Allan Berggren for the following information\n\nThe Ashuelot is the quintessential 'step-up-to-Class III' stream. It has 3-ft. drops, sometimes in complex, with plenty of recovery pools. It has, in addition, a Class IV Surprise at the top and a IV- gorge at the bottom (at low levels). At splashing 7 on the Gilsum gauge, Surprise has monster holes, and the gorge goes thrashing around a corner, splits over a knife-edge, etc., etc. What remained was four miles of quite continuous Class IV action, with big holes, sometimes 3/4 the width of the stream, and complex pourover-mixmaster combos which made us shift from 'let's find a feature' mode to something more timid--'maybe I can afford to catch the edge of this hole, curl the edge of that drop.' I have just bought an Ultraclean-- However, in this water, the decks are constantly awash, and the ends rarely out of water. I found myself slicing through the far side of holes going downstream, being frequently held in suspension over drops, buried in bubbles up to my navel, and having to paddle briskly leaning foreward to avoid being backendered more than I already was. Ryan found the run at this level a memorable experience, and it was a kick to see him disappear over standing waves, ledge drops, and to see him struggle through the 10-yd foam piles in the narrow places. One curl thrust me about 5 ft to the right in one shot.\n\nNote: You can avoid the two toughest sections of this river (Surprise and Gilsum gorge)and cut the trip down to 3.5 miles. Put in about 1/2 downstream of Surprise rapid, (you will also avoid some flatwater here). Take out well above the gorge at an old lumber yard on river left. Gilsum gorge is considered class V in very high water and a difficult Class IV+ in high water.\n\nGilsum Gorge also has a keeper hole at low flow, so all paddlers should be wary and scout this carefully before entering it.\n\n### Technical info\n\n```\nPut in elevation........1069'\nTake out elevation......731'\nTotal drop..............338'\nAverage drop/mile.......64'.......Including Surprise and Gilsum gorge\nDistance................5.3 miles\n1st mile drop...........52'......Includes Surprise rapid\n2nd mile drop...........30'\n3rd mile drop...........75'\n4th mile drop...........69'\n5th mile drop...........85'......Includes upper Gulsum gorge\n5.3 mile drop...........27'......(90' average) includes lower Gilsum gorge\nRiver width average.....25'\nRiver geology...........Small to medium schist boulders, some ledges\nRiver water quality.....Good, clarity average\nScenery.................Nice river valley usually within view of\n                         route 10, a few homes on the banks.\nWildlife................Occasional deer, hawks\n```\n\n## Directions\n\n### Put in\n\nFrom Keene NH to NH state route 10/12/9 north.\n\nApproximately 3 miles take a left (north) on NH 10.\n\nApproximately 10 miles look for Surprise rapid on your left shortly after crossing over the Marlow town line.\n\n### Take out\n\nHead back south on NH 10 approximately 3 miles.\n\nPark and take out shortly after the first or second river crossings (first one easier).\n\nIf you are running Gilsum gorge, take a right at the Gilsum bridge 1.25 miles until the river comes into view with a private bridge crossing.","averageGradient":"64","maxGradient":"90","length":"5.6","correlations":[{"reachID":"1159","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"260","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"1700"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"ASHUELOT RIVER NEAR GILSUM, NH","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"01157000","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"103","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"3.04","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"43.03924586","longitude":"-72.2700858"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/01157000","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/01157000/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"103","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:30:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["108846","nw8Q8Cq6aDwtYtJeOfuag","AJ9A58fzdcvwBdhM7QDLR","t3vcsuoXESRsJSgiPAGEH","bRbfI9y7rFBgIP5saepR0","114082"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-72.211305,43.078341],[-72.212188,43.077112],[-72.212518,43.07653],[-72.212906,43.076004],[-72.212975,43.075982],[-72.212965,43.075929],[-72.213548,43.075227],[-72.21382,43.074862],[-72.213968,43.07471],[-72.215075,43.074013],[-72.215528,43.073763],[-72.215957,43.073562],[-72.21624,43.073384],[-72.216371,43.07325],[-72.216442,43.07313],[-72.216546,43.072731],[-72.216574,43.072669],[-72.216688,43.072528],[-72.216898,43.072342],[-72.217817,43.071601],[-72.218389,43.071154],[-72.218659,43.070999],[-72.21912,43.070841],[-72.21993,43.070685],[-72.220256,43.070604],[-72.220607,43.070458],[-72.221052,43.070149],[-72.221533,43.069675],[-72.222223,43.068906],[-72.222323,43.068771],[-72.222487,43.0685],[-72.222555,43.068264],[-72.222576,43.068024],[-72.222583,43.06717],[-72.222626,43.066876],[-72.222836,43.066487],[-72.223017,43.06615],[-72.22311,43.065885],[-72.223173,43.06503],[-72.223119,43.063923],[-72.223169,43.063556],[-72.223237,43.063405],[-72.22333,43.063255],[-72.223639,43.062946],[-72.223879,43.062765],[-72.224351,43.062461],[-72.224787,43.062269],[-72.225262,43.062172],[-72.225514,43.062176],[-72.225764,43.062053],[-72.225924,43.061974],[-72.22634,43.06169],[-72.226867,43.061383],[-72.227098,43.061306],[-72.227438,43.061268],[-72.227539,43.061249],[-72.227688,43.061197],[-72.228104,43.061163],[-72.228769,43.060916],[-72.22931,43.060776],[-72.230246,43.060549],[-72.230995,43.060448],[-72.232053,43.060409],[-72.232422,43.060371],[-72.233334,43.060368],[-72.23429,43.060393],[-72.23488